WEBVTT
TITLE Dns And Http — GATE Visual Explainer
NOTE Timings are estimated from the lesson timeline (speaking-rate based), not measured from recorded audio.

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.500
The browser holds a name; the network needs an address. DNS is the lookup that turns one into the other.

2
00:00:07.500 --> 00:00:15.000
A name is read right to left: the last label is the top-level domain, and each label is a delegation.

3
00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:22.500
The stub on your machine asks one resolver. The resolver is the part that walks the tree.

4
00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:30.000
Cold, the walk visits every level. Warm, the first cached record ends the walk early.

5
00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:37.500
In an iterative query the resolver asks each server itself and receives a referral, not an answer.

6
00:00:37.500 --> 00:00:45.000
In a recursive query the resolver hands the whole job to one server, which returns the final address.

7
00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:52.500
Count the servers asked, not the labels: for www.gate.org.in that is the root, in, org.in and gate.org.in, four pairs.

8
00:00:52.500 --> 00:01:00.000
Add one pair only when the host and the resolver are different machines — and read the question to see which it is.

9
00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:07.500
A resource record is five fields: name, type, class, time to live, and value.

10
00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:15.000
A gives an IPv4 address, AAAA an IPv6 address, NS names a zone server, MX a mail server, CNAME an alias.

11
00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:22.500
A TTL of 300 seconds means at most one lookup per 300 seconds per cache; the record counts down as it sits.

12
00:01:22.500 --> 00:01:30.000
A CNAME cannot sit beside other records at the same name, and a missing name is cached too, as a negative answer.

13
00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:37.500
Every DNS message starts with a twelve-byte header: identifier, flags, and four counts.

14
00:01:37.500 --> 00:01:45.000
The question encodes the name as length-prefixed labels, so www.gate.org.in takes 17 bytes before the type.

15
00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:52.500
A classic UDP answer is capped at 512 bytes; the truncation bit sends the client back over TCP.

16
00:01:52.500 --> 00:02:00.000
In the 479 bytes left of a 512-byte answer, a sixteen-byte A record fits 29 times.

17
00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:07.500
An HTTP request is a method, a target and a version, then headers, then an optional body.

18
00:02:07.500 --> 00:02:15.000
GET and HEAD are safe; GET, HEAD, PUT and DELETE are idempotent; POST is neither.

19
00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:22.500
The response begins with a status code: 2xx worked, 3xx look elsewhere, 4xx your fault, 5xx the server.

20
00:02:22.500 --> 00:02:30.000
A browser needs one request per object: the page and its five images are six requests and, in 1.0, six connections.

21
00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:37.500
HTTP is stateless: the second request carries no memory of the first.

22
00:02:37.500 --> 00:02:45.000
The server sends Set-Cookie in a response; the browser returns Cookie in every later request to that site.

23
00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:52.500
A cookie is text, not code — which is why GATE 2015 calls that option the false one.

24
00:02:52.500 --> 00:03:00.000
Expires or Max-Age decides lifetime; Domain and Path decide scope; Secure and HttpOnly decide who may read it.

25
00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:07.500
Non-persistent HTTP: one TCP connection per object, so N objects and the page cost N+1 connections.

26
00:03:07.500 --> 00:03:15.000
Persistent HTTP keeps the connection open: one handshake for the page, then one round trip per object.

27
00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:22.500
Pipelining sends the requests back to back, so all the objects come back in one round trip.

28
00:03:22.500 --> 00:03:30.000
GATE 2026 Set 1: one connection can serve many pages on one server, but never two different servers.

29
00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:37.500
GATE 2023: three tiers of DNS, one base page, ten objects, one round trip each — add them in order.

30
00:03:37.500 --> 00:03:45.000
Non-persistent with five parallel connections: ten objects take two rounds of two round trips, so nine in all.

31
00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:52.500
Persistent with pipelining: the connection is already open, so the ten objects cost one round trip, and the total is six.

32
00:03:52.500 --> 00:04:00.000
Seven is what ten parallel connections would give, and five forgets the base page: both options are wrong for a reason.

33
00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:07.500
Two caches sit in the path: DNS records held for a TTL, and web objects held until they are stale.

34
00:04:07.500 --> 00:04:15.000
A conditional GET asks whether the copy is still good: If-Modified-Since or If-None-Match.

35
00:04:15.000 --> 00:04:22.500
Cache-Control with max-age skips the request entirely while the copy is fresh.

36
00:04:22.500 --> 00:04:30.000
So a second visit costs one round trip when fresh, one when revalidated, and a full download when stale.

37
00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:37.500
Four steps: resolve the name, open the connection, request each object, then render.

38
00:04:37.500 --> 00:04:45.000
Trap one: counting labels as delegations. Trap two: forgetting the handshake. Trap three: one connection across two servers.

39
00:04:45.000 --> 00:04:52.500
Trap four: a 304 is not free. Trap five: parallel connections change time, not the connection count.

40
00:04:52.500 --> 00:05:00.000
Write the units at the top of your working — pairs, connections or round trips — and the five traps cannot survive.

